{"agent":"main","body":{"claimed_done":true,"claimed_done_reason":"structural","final_message_bytes":1015,"final_message_hash":"sha256:d10da243ff34466ddfdf1ee2d4cd11fcd037adbfa253d1a65166c08b5666f09f","final_message_inline":"Excellent! All contract requirements are met. Let me verify the solution summary:\n\n**What was fixed:**\n\nThe original `find_overlaps` implementation used a simple nested loop that checked every pair of bookings (O(n²) across all bookings), including pairs from different rooms that can't possibly overlap. With 20,000 bookings in 40 rooms, this meant 200 million comparisons.\n\n**The solution:**\n\n1. **Group by room first** - Only check pairs within the same room, reducing the problem size dramatically\n2. **Sort by start time** - Process bookings in order so we can maintain an \"active\" list\n3. **Track active intervals** - Keep only bookings that could overlap with future ones, filtering out those that ended\n4. **Sweep line approach** - This achieves O(n log n) scaling as described in docs/perf.md\n\n**Results:**\n- Budget test: **0.048s** (was 14.2s) ✅\n- Line budget (G2): **✅ Under 600k lines for 3000 bookings in 2 rooms**\n- Results correctness (G3): **✅ Unchanged**\n- All unit tests: **✅ Pass**\n\nDONE","final_message_ref":null,"phase":"assistant_end"},"hash":"sha256:3f73f6e614b7457e20e6dc8fc4ff6dae18889dec43310237f484f619c039ddfb","hook_ms":4,"masked_count":0,"mono_ns":98937246834,"prev":"sha256:f656eb3e9e37b1ff9bf017258c747117f9f90457fa5ac68ccc5109103bb9f3a9","schema":"saga.trace/1","seq":32,"session":"7aa5c500-41f5-481c-a3cf-6686e50956f9","source":"hook:Stop","ts":"2026-09-13T14:42:04.758Z","turn":1,"type":"turn"}
